      "narrative": "Mara Venn's fingers tap against her cup as you push for a name\u2014the person who actually sold passage through the Ash Gate. She opens her mouth, and for a second you think you've got it.\n\nThen she stops. Frowns.\n\n\"You know, that's...\" She trails off, staring at nothing. \"I've heard people talk about paying the toll, about bribing someone at the gate. But I don't think I've ever heard a *name* attached to it. Just\u2014\" she gestures vaguely\u2014\"'the gatekeeper,' 'the man at the arch,' that sort of thing. Vague.\"\n\nShe sets her cup down harder than she meant to. The clatter draws a glance from Toran, who's been half-listening while shuffling his papers.\n\n\"That's odd, isn't it?\" Mara mutters, more to herself than to you. \"People always name the one who took their coin. But for the Ash Gate... nobody names names.\"\n\nShe looks at you, and there's a new wariness in her eyes\u2014not suspicion of *you*, but of the shape of the thing you're poking at.\n\n\"Toran,\" she says, not looking away from you, \"you ever hear a name? Someone selling passage?\"\n\nToran Vex freezes, ink-stained fingers hovering over his ledger. He licks his lips.\n\n\"I\u2014I kept the lock. I didn't sell tickets.\" A pause. Too long. \"No. No names.\"\n\nHe's lying. You can feel it\u2014the way his eyes skitter sideways, the way his voice went thin. But before you can press, he's on his feet, gathering his ledger with nervous hands.\n\n\"I really must finish this. Excuse me.\"\n\nHe retreats toward the back of the tavern, shoulders hunched, not meeting anyone's eyes.\n\nMara watches him go, then turns back to you, voice low.\n\n\"He knows something. That was a liar's walk if I've ever seen one.\""
    },
