      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the hiss of lamps and the low murmur of patrons. The morning air hits you\u2014damp, carrying the smell of wet stone and something older, like rust and river silt.\n\nThe road leads north out of the town's sparse edge, past shuttered workshops and a single cart track that dissolves into mud. And then you see it.\n\nThe Ash Gate.\n\nIt rises from the flat ground like a broken tooth\u2014a cracked black arch, maybe fifteen feet high, standing alone against a grey sky. No wall flanks it. No road passes through it. It's just *there*, a door to nowhere, set in a patch of churned earth where grass refuses to grow.\n\nTo the north of the arch, as Mara said, an alcove has been built into the hillside\u2014a lean-to of grey stone and weathered timber, its doorway curtained with oilcloth. A thin trickle of smoke rises from a metal pipe jutting from the roof.\n\nA figure sits on a stool outside the alcove, hunched over a workbench cluttered with tools and metal shavings. She's maybe fifty, with grey-streaked hair pulled back tight, and she's filing something small and brass with the slow, deliberate patience of someone who has done this for decades.\n\nShe doesn't look up as you approach. But her hands stop moving.\n\n\"You're not Guild,\" she says. Flat. Not a question."
