      "narrative": "The name leaves your mouth before you've fully decided to speak it\u2014*Old Bram*\u2014carrying across the empty field like a stone dropped into still water.\n\nIt echoes. Briefly. The Ash Gate swallows the sound.\n\nSilence answers. The kind of silence that has weight, that presses against your ears. No birds. No wind through grass. Just the faint, distant creak of something\u2014the gate itself, maybe, settling in its arch.\n\nBehind you, the file scrapes against brass. Once. Then stops.\n\n\"You're looking for a dead man.\"\n\nSable Quill hasn't turned around. She's still hunched over her workbench, but her hands are still now, the file resting across her knee.\n\n\"Old Bram's been dead six weeks. Drowned in the river, they say. His boat\u2014the *Grey Hind*\u2014still rots at the landing, half-sunk.\" She sets the file down with a deliberate click and finally turns to face you. Her eyes are pale, the colour of river stones. \"He was the last one who worked the gate. Took the tolls. Knew the passage.\"\n\nShe studies you.\n\n\"Why are you calling for a dead man at a sealed gate?\""
