      "narrative": "You drop to a crouch beneath the black arch, running your fingers through the dust and debris that gather at its base. Dead leaves. Grit. A few small stones. You brush the surface aside, squinting at the earth beneath, searching for\u2014something. A seam. A stain. A scrape that shouldn't be there.\n\nNothing.\n\nThe ground is undisturbed. Compacted, yes\u2014old dirt packed hard by years of feet and weather\u2014but there's no fresh turning, no hidden cache, no outline of a buried shape. Just dirt. Just dust.\n\nBehind you, the woman on the stool watches. She hasn't moved.\n\n\"You expecting to find something under there?\" Sable Quill's voice carries a dry amusement, but not a warm one. \"Buried coin? A body? The gate's secrets don't live in the dirt, stranger.\"\n\nShe picks up her file again, turning a small brass component over in her fingers.\n\n\"They live in the lock. And the lock's been silent for thirty years.\""
