      },
      "narrative": "The walk back to the Copper Finch passes under a sky that's beginning to spit a thin, cold drizzle. By the time you push through the tavern door, the rain has beaded on your shoulders and the warmth hits you like a wall\u2014woodsmoke, lamp oil, old ale.\n\nThe place has thinned out. A few heads turn at your entrance, then go back to their cups. The fire crackles low in the hearth.\n\nYour old table\u2014the scarred one near the back wall, where you sat with Mara\u2014is empty. The mugs have been cleared. A wax stain marks where her cup sat.\n\nToran Vex is nowhere in sight. His usual corner near the bar is vacant, his ledger absent. The door to the back room is shut.\n\nBut Mara Venn is still here.\n\nShe sits at the bar, a fresh cup of something steaming in front of her, her charcoal coat draped over the stool beside her. She's reading a folded letter by the light of a copper lamp, her brow furrowed.\n\nShe glances up as you cross the room. Her expression shifts\u2014curious, but guarded.\n\n\"You look like you walked through a ghost.\" She folds the letter and tucks it into her coat. \"Find anything at the gate?\""
